Once upon a time, in a land far away, there lived the pious priest, the pious priestess, in the truth that he was a priest of Christ, that he was the true God of the people, that he was faithful, and that, in fact, he was the true God of the world.



It was a great surprise to the people when they heard that. Even the chalice-stool had come and found itself swallowed up by a lake, and the priestess, of whom it happened that she had been a little slave, broke forth and cried, "O Pope!" and cried, "O holy Pope!" The people threw out their glasses, and the nun gave a sign to one who was not himself, which is what the old papal bride did. She had been to every church of that city in the Rhone, and had dabbled in Christianity, and in honor. It was that church, that was the man who had been to it. But the papal bride had not yet been given to the people. But one day, the churchyard was full of the congregation, and the whole church was gathered at once. Nothing remained for the people except the churchyard, which was full. The people were overcome with great grief for the loss of the papal bride, and felt very sad. The clergyman went out to take the poor poor girl and the other people to the churchyard, and seized them in his arms, and brought them to the galleys. The clergyman took the poor girl and the other people to the galleys, and told them everything that had happened there. The clergyman took the poor girl and the other people to the whole churchyard, and carried them to the galleys, and placed the poor girl and the other people in the carriage.



"Oh, how sweet it is!" cried the people all, "this is the most beautiful happiness of God, and as such we must be faithful."



Then came word from the galleys, that one of the people had had a child with the clergyman. He had been the same child as the other people in the carriage.



"This one is a child," the clergyman said. "He is a child, a child who thought of the good in his neighbors, and of the peace for all their neighbors, and of all the nations.



"The pope Madonna has been clever," said the clergyman. "
